ing (crew cooperation), but difficulty communicating ideas (communication), egocentric (maybe attitude), not willing to further education (adaptability). No stress resilience evidence.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality, safety), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation), but unfriendly (crew cooperation), eats unhealthily (health maybe impact). No stress resilience evidence.\n\nWe need to weigh. The panel hasn't discussed A and C yet. We could bring A's strengths and concerns, compare to D and B. Perhaps A's quick decision making and concern for others could outweigh D's interpersonal issues? But A's communication difficulty and egocentric may be problematic.\n\nC has strong technical and leadership but unfriendly and health concerns.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, crew cooperation and communication are crucial. Both D and B have interpersonal issues. B's uncooperative and grumpy may be more severe than D's arrogance? D's arrogance and loner may also be severe. Which is more manageable? Hard to say.\n\nWe need to make a recommendation. Perhaps we could argue that Candidate B's stress handling and conscientiousness make him more reliable, and his interpersonal issues are less severe than D's arrogance and moody, which could affect safety. So we might vote B.\n\nAlternatively, we could argue that Candidate A's quick decision making and concern for others is valuable, and his communication difficulty may be mitigated, and his egocentric attitude is less severe than D's arrogance. But A lacks stress resilience evidence.\n\nGiven the current evidence, I think B is still strongest overall, despite cooperation concerns, because reliability and stress handling are critical for long-distance flights. D's interpersonal issues may be more problematic for crew coordination.\n\nThus I will vote Candidate B, and explain.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: Agent 1 now votes D, Agent 2 votes B. I will argue for B, referencing B's conscientiousness, stress handling, technical competence, and note that his uncooperative behavior is a concern but perhaps less severe than D's arrogance and moody, loner traits, and also note that D lacks evidence of stress resilience.\n\nWe also need to bring in maybe a fact about D's lack of stress resilience evidence.\n\nWe must not ask tool calls unless needed. We have enough.\n\nThus produce public message: state vote B, reference discussion, give evidence, explain."}
